The most common type of IMs is the squirrel-cage IM.
It consists of:
o a stator incorporated with three-phase armature
windings;
o a rotor incorporated with cage bars that are
short-circuited by two end-rings;
o two end-bearings to support the rotor;
o a machine frame with two end-bells to house the
machine.

- Rs and Xs are respectively the winding resistance
and leakage reactance in the stator;
- Rr and Xr are respectively the winding resistance and
leakage reactance in the rotor at standstill;
- Es and Er are respectively the induced EMF in the
stator and induced EMF in the rotor at standstill;
- Rm and Xm are respectively the core-loss resistance
and magnetizing reactance in the stator;
- k is the ratio of the number of turns of stator winding
to the number of turns of rotor winding.

The advantages of field-oriented control can be summarized as follows:
- High efficiency obtained by aligning the stator and rotor flows to generate excellent torque
production by the machine;
- High dynamic response obtained by controlling the quantities (voltages, currents and fluxes)
in module and phase;
- Low torque ripple, as the stator field is continuously updated according to the position of the
rotor field;
- Independent control of flux and torque, as in direct current machines;
- High dynamic range through weakening operation;
- High torque and low starting current.
